1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,187
Welcome to Module Twenty-Three, and to Unit Eight: fitness assays.

2
00:00:05,187 --> 00:00:09,525
Think about the last time you wanted to know whether two genotypes truly differed.

3
00:00:09,525 --> 00:00:13,575
You didn't trust a survey of wild plants growing in different places.

4
00:00:13,575 --> 00:00:16,687
You grew them side by side in a common garden.

5
00:00:16,687 --> 00:00:20,112
Same soil, same water, same season.

6
00:00:20,112 --> 00:00:21,687
Replicated plots.

7
00:00:21,687 --> 00:00:25,250
And a fitness measure you fixed before the first seed went in.

8
00:00:25,250 --> 00:00:28,687
An eval is the common garden for an AI system.

9
00:00:28,687 --> 00:00:32,237
You hold the tasks, the grader and the setup constant.

10
00:00:32,237 --> 00:00:36,562
You change one thing, maybe a prompt, a model or a skill file.

11
00:00:36,562 --> 00:00:37,875
Then you measure.

12
00:00:37,875 --> 00:00:42,412
Until now, almost everything you have built in this course has been judged by eye.

13
00:00:42,412 --> 00:00:45,025
From today, it gets an assay.

14
00:00:45,025 --> 00:00:49,425
Anthropic's guide to eval design gives four properties of a good one.

15
00:00:49,425 --> 00:00:53,812
The tasks should look like your real work, not like whatever was easy to write.

16
00:00:53,812 --> 00:00:56,125
Stronger models should score higher.

17
00:00:56,125 --> 00:01:00,100
There should be headroom, so the best system still misses some items.

18
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:03,275
And the score should not jump around from run to run.

19
00:01:03,275 --> 00:01:06,375
There is a sampling trap here that you will recognise.

20
00:01:06,375 --> 00:01:13,312
If you build your test set from the cases today's model gets wrong, you are sampling the valleys of one model's abilities.

21
00:01:13,312 --> 00:01:18,425
Your eval ends up describing that model's weak spots, not what is hard in your field.

22
00:01:18,425 --> 00:01:24,637
So choose hard cases because you, the expert, judge them hard, and be able to say why.

23
00:01:24,637 --> 00:01:26,437
Then comes hill-climbing.

24
00:01:26,437 --> 00:01:30,162
Change one thing, re-run, keep it if the score improves.

25
00:01:30,162 --> 00:01:32,612
It is the same search you met on day one.

26
00:01:32,612 --> 00:01:35,125
But now the optimiser can overfit.

27
00:01:35,125 --> 00:01:38,825
It can tune itself to the quirks of your particular test items.

28
00:01:38,825 --> 00:01:41,175
The defence is a held-out split.

29
00:01:41,175 --> 00:01:46,262
One set of items the optimiser may study, and another it never sees.

30
00:01:46,262 --> 00:01:51,487
If the training score rises while the held-out score stays flat, you revert the change.

31
00:01:51,487 --> 00:01:53,312
And remember noise.

32
00:01:53,312 --> 00:01:57,775
With ten held-out items, one item is ten percentage points.

33
00:01:57,775 --> 00:02:00,412
A small gain may be nothing but chance.

34
00:02:00,412 --> 00:02:05,325
Report a confidence interval, and only trust improvements bigger than your noise.

35
00:02:05,325 --> 00:02:14,325
In the lab you will write eight study descriptions from your own field, labelled by design: experiment, observational, model or synthesis.

36
00:02:14,325 --> 00:02:25,437
Your coding agent will add more, you will check every label, and then it will run three careful rounds of hill-climbing on a prompt, with a train and test split and intervals on every score.

37
00:02:25,437 --> 00:02:26,750
Keep that folder.

38
00:02:26,750 --> 00:02:31,750
It comes back in the next two modules, and again when you fine-tune your own small model.

39
00:02:31,750 --> 00:02:33,987
Start with Part A on your phone.

40
00:02:33,987 --> 00:02:38,800
Write your eight items, and for each tricky one, write down why it is hard.

